{
  "image_name": "0b747c53621c3d53260ef2a48cd1236f.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how telomeres are elongated during the cell cycle and how that reaction is shut off. In S phase, shelterin proteins bound at the chromosome end recruit telomerase through the TPP1–POT1 region, with TPP1 shown contacting telomerase and helping position it at the single-stranded telomeric overhang. Telomerase is depicted as an RNA–protein complex whose RNA template aligns with the chromosome end.\n\nOnce recruited, telomerase extends the 3′ G-rich strand by adding repeated telomeric DNA. The diagram emphasizes increased processivity during this extension step, meaning multiple repeats can be added before telomerase dissociates. Shelterin remains associated with the telomere during this synthesis, suggesting it supports efficient repeat addition rather than simply marking the site.\n\nIn late S/G2 phase, the extension reaction is terminated and telomerase is no longer active at the end. A CST complex containing CTC1, STN1, and TEN1 is then recruited, where it appears to block further telomerase action and promote fill-in synthesis by DNA polymerase α–primase. This final step restores the complementary C-strand, leaving a regulated telomeric overhang after elongation.",
